      "writtenOn": "2021-08-04T13:07:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In fact, any test using this method needs to be aware of how many requests it will be filing in the runnable, because it needs to leave that many slots open for legitimate requests. Debugging this, I came to the horrified realization that\n• All three users of this method happen to file exactly one additional request in the runnable they pass.\n• This method was written thinking it was filling the request counter, but was actually leaving one slot open because of a fencepost bug (see \"\u003c maxCount - 1\" in the old code).\nThe two unnoticed issues were cancelling each other out.\n\nI initially made an overload that passes 1 since that\u0027s what all existing callers do, but finally decided against since really users have to care about this. I guess you could argue that if we made 0 the default value, users who need to pass something else would see the test fail anyway, but I thought it was more friendly to force them to think about it upfront than to let them crash and leave them to figure out as investigation of these counts is tricky.\n\nIf we\u0027re not going to make this argument optional, then it\u0027s generally more readable to leave the lambda at the end.",
      "parentUuid": "b26117ae_4162d709",
